# Vyom --- 10-Year Business Plan

**Version:** Generic Vertical AI Operating System\
**Date:** 29 October 2026\
**Status:** Strategic thesis and planning assumptions --- market,
pricing and financials require validation

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 1. Executive summary

Vyom is an AI-native operating system for complex, document-heavy,
rules-driven business workflows. It turns unstructured information into
structured records, guided decisions, controlled workflows and auditable
outcomes.

The company is designed around a reusable platform rather than a single
industry application. A common foundation handles ingestion, extraction,
evidence, workflow state, rules, approvals, integrations, security and
auditability. Industry-specific modules add the terminology, schemas,
decision policies, forms, integrations and controls required by each
market.

**The thesis:** Many regulated and operationally complex industries
still depend on people moving information between documents, email,
spreadsheets and legacy systems. General-purpose AI can read and draft,
but enterprises need more than a chatbot: they need predictable workflow
execution, traceable evidence, permissioning, exception handling and
accountable human decisions.

Vyom aims to provide that missing operational layer.

### The strategy in one page

-   **Platform:** One reusable workflow and governance engine, with
    industry-specific modules.
-   **Initial market:** Select one narrow, high-cost workflow in a
    single industry after customer discovery. Pharmacovigilance remains
    a candidate wedge, not the definition of the entire company.
-   **Expansion:** Prove one workflow, reuse the platform for adjacent
    workflows in the same industry, then enter another vertical only
    when the shared foundation creates a measurable cost or delivery
    advantage.
-   **Product principle:** AI interprets and proposes; deterministic
    code and configured rules validate; authorized people approve
    consequential decisions.
-   **Business model:** Annual SaaS contracts, usage-based pricing,
    enterprise plans and limited implementation services that improve
    the core product.
-   **Current stage:** No external funding assumed. Validate buyer
    urgency, access to representative data, willingness to pay and
    measurable workflow improvement before committing to a large build
    or full-time transition.

**Long-term ambition:** Build a durable enterprise software company
whose defensibility comes from workflow orchestration, domain
configurations, evaluation data, integrations, governance and customer
trust---not from owning a single foundation model.

A large outcome is possible but not guaranteed. The company should
optimize first for repeatable customer value, retention, software gross
margin and expansion revenue rather than a headline valuation target.

