## 2. The problem

Across regulated and document-heavy industries, important work often
follows the same pattern:

1.  Information arrives in inconsistent formats.
2.  Staff read, classify and re-enter information manually.
3.  Facts must be matched to entities, policies, records or standards.
4.  Rules and judgment are applied across several systems.
5.  Exceptions require follow-up and approval.
6.  A final decision, report, filing or transaction must be produced.
7.  The organization must prove what happened, why it happened and who
    approved it.

This creates recurring problems:

-   **Fragmented information:** Documents, email, spreadsheets, portals
    and databases do not share a consistent data model.
-   **Manual processing:** Trained employees spend time on extraction,
    reconciliation, drafting and routing instead of judgment-heavy work.
-   **Inconsistent decisions:** Policies are interpreted differently
    across teams and vendors.
-   **Weak traceability:** It can be difficult to reconstruct the
    evidence, rules, model version and approvals behind an outcome.
-   **Slow exception resolution:** Missing data and conflicting evidence
    lead to long follow-up cycles.
-   **Expensive change:** New rules, formats or business processes
    require repeated manual updates and integration work.
-   **Untrusted automation:** A generic AI assistant may produce a
    plausible answer but cannot, by itself, guarantee completeness,
    repeatability, access control or an auditable action history.

Vyom is not intended to replace every enterprise system or automate
every decision. It is designed to coordinate a well-defined workflow
across existing systems, with the right degree of automation and human
control.

## 3. Why now

Several technology and market changes make this approach more practical:

-   **Models can work with mixed inputs.** Modern models can extract
    information from documents, classify content, draft summaries and
    reason over context.
-   **Inference is becoming more accessible.** Multiple model providers
    and open models make it possible to select an appropriate cost,
    latency and privacy profile.
-   **Agentic systems can use tools.** Models can call APIs and trigger
    workflow steps, provided that execution is bounded by explicit
    permissions and validation.
-   **Enterprises still require control.** As AI adoption grows, buyers
    need evaluation, monitoring, access control, audit trails, data
    boundaries and human approval.
-   **Integration remains difficult.** Enterprise value depends on
    connecting AI to the actual systems and procedures where work
    happens, not just generating text.
-   **Industry-specific AI is emerging.** Specialized products can
    outperform generic assistants when they encode domain workflows,
    data structures and acceptance criteria.

These are strategic observations, not proof that any particular customer
segment is ready to buy. Vyom must validate demand and purchasing cycles
through interviews and paid pilots.

