## 9. Go-to-market

### Phase 1 --- Founder-led discovery and paid design partners

-   Select one workflow and one buyer profile.
-   Interview 15--20 target buyers and frontline users.
-   Obtain authorized sample data and document baseline metrics.
-   Secure one or two paid design partners.
-   Build only the workflow needed to test the core value proposition.

### Phase 2 --- Repeatability

-   Turn pilot-specific configuration into reusable module capabilities.
-   Demonstrate reliable performance across different datasets and
    customer conditions.
-   Document deployment, security, support and change-management
    procedures.
-   Convert pilots into annual contracts.
-   Build a reference customer and a concise ROI case study, with
    permission.

### Phase 3 --- Focused vertical expansion

-   Add adjacent workflows bought by the same department or
    organization.
-   Use the same identity, permissions, audit and integration
    foundation.
-   Partner with specialist consultancies, BPOs, systems integrators or
    industry vendors where they provide distribution.
-   Expand only when support and implementation are repeatable.

### Phase 4 --- Second vertical

-   Reuse the core platform but build a separate domain module and
    validation pack.
-   Assign a domain expert and buyer-specific go-to-market owner.
-   Require evidence that a meaningful share of platform components can
    be reused.
-   Do not let second-vertical work slow down retention and product
    quality in the first vertical.

### Sales principles

-   Start with a bounded operational pain, not a broad AI transformation
    pitch.
-   Sell to both the economic buyer and the people doing the work.
-   Agree on data access, security, baseline and success criteria before
    building.
-   Expect enterprise procurement, security review and integration work
    to extend sales cycles.
-   Use channel partners only when their economics and customer access
    are demonstrated.

