## 13. Ten-year roadmap: 2026--2036

The roadmap is milestone-based, not calendar-guaranteed. A failed gate
extends the phase.

### Phase 0 --- Validate the wedge (first 90 days)

-   Compare two or three candidate industries and workflows.
-   Interview 15--20 buyers and frontline users.
-   Select one workflow using the scorecard.
-   Secure authorized data and a domain expert.
-   Define baseline metrics and pilot acceptance criteria.
-   Target one or two paid pilots before major investment.

**Gate 0:** Evidence of a buyer, budget, accessible data, measurable
value and a credible path to paid deployment.

### Phase 1 --- Deliver one reliable workflow (months 4--12)

-   Build ingestion, extraction, validation, human review, audit and
    output.
-   Run a bounded pilot and compare with baseline.
-   Validate performance on representative data.
-   Convert at least one pilot to recurring revenue.
-   Document security, deployment and support processes.

**Gate 1:** Customer-accepted performance, a referenceable outcome and
evidence that the workflow can be deployed again.

### Phase 2 --- Productize the first module (years 2--3)

-   Replace one-off code with reusable configurations.
-   Add required integrations and administration.
-   Improve onboarding, monitoring and unit economics.
-   Win multiple customers with substantially similar workflows.
-   Establish a repeatable sales and implementation playbook.

**Gate 2:** Repeatable deployments, retention evidence, healthy
gross-margin direction and a product-led share of delivery.

### Phase 3 --- Expand within the first vertical (years 3--5)

-   Add adjacent workflows that share buyers, data and controls.
-   Expand through referrals and carefully selected channel partners.
-   Establish enterprise security and quality capabilities.
-   Reduce custom implementation effort per customer.

**Gate 3:** The first vertical is a durable business with strong
retention and an expansion path.

### Phase 4 --- Launch a second vertical (years 5--7)

-   Select a second industry based on validated demand and platform
    reuse.
-   Build a separate domain module, evaluation pack and compliance map.
-   Run paid design partnerships before broad launch.
-   Keep vertical-specific assumptions and unit economics distinct.

**Gate 4:** Evidence that the second module can sell and deploy without
materially weakening the first vertical.

### Phase 5 --- Multi-vertical platform (years 7--10)

-   Offer shared administration, integration and governance across
    modules.
-   Establish partner distribution and enterprise account expansion.
-   Develop a module marketplace or partner ecosystem only if demand
    supports it.
-   Expand internationally when compliance, support and sales economics
    justify it.
-   Invest in deeper workflow analytics and bounded autonomous
    execution.

**2036 ambition:** A multi-vertical enterprise AI company with recurring
revenue, strong retention and repeatable module economics. The exact
scale depends on customer evidence, not on the roadmap alone.

